// Congruence, form 2 of 2: THROUGH THUNKS (ADR-76; the proposal's "identity
// of computations goes through thunks, as everything does in CBPV").
//
// For a defined function `double`, the two APPLICATIONS `double(x)` and
// `double(y)` are computations -- they have no value-level identity. Their
// THUNKS are values, so from `p : Path(Integer, x, y)` one `walk` derives
//
//     Path(U(F(Integer)), thunk { double(x) }, thunk { double(y) })
//
// -- the two suspended calls are equal AS THUNKS. This is deliberately
// intensional (no funext, ever): the proof identifies the two suspensions
// because their bodies are equal term-for-term after transport, not because
// they "behave the same". Claiming more -- congruence of the RESULTS of an
// arbitrary effectful `f` -- is not derivable, and this family never claims
// it.
//
// The motive's endpoints are compound (thunk literals), so it uses the
// walk-form identity former `Path(?, ..., ...)` exactly as `cong-value.gandr`;
// the base's witness thunk infers its own type (`U(F(Integer))`), no
// ascription needed.

def double(x: Integer) -> F Integer {
  ret (x + x)
}

def cong_double = thunk {
  fn(p: Path(Integer, 4, 4)) {
    walk(p,
      fn(x, y, q) { Path(?, thunk { double(x) }, thunk { double(y) }) },
      fn(x) { ret here(thunk { double(x) }) })
  }
};

cong_double(here(4))

// The harness checks:
//@ expect: clean
//@ expect-def: double
//@ expect-def: cong_double
//@ expect-last-value: here(<thunk>)
